    <example workspace="1in">
        <p>
           The graphical representation of four relationships is given below.  Are these relationship functions? Why?  For those examples that are functions state the domain and range of the function.   
        </p>
        <sidebyside>
            <image source="fcngraph1.png" width="40%">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a line</shortdescription>
            </image>
            <image source="fcngraph2.png" width="40%">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a radical function</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </sidebyside>
        <sidebyside>
            <image source="fcngraph3.png" width="40%">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a circle</shortdescription>
            </image>
            <image source="fcngraph4.png" width="40%">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a parabola</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </sidebyside>
    </example>
    <p>
       <alert>Vertical Line Test</alert> - A relation is a function if and only if every vertical line intersects the graph of the relation at no more than one point.  
    </p>
    <p>
        <alert>Function notation</alert> - The equation form of a function is referred to as function notation.  In function notation we write the output variable followed by the input variable in parentheses.  
        As an example consider the equation <m>R(p)=-2.4p^2+216p</m> which relates revenue of an item to price.  We read <m>R(p)</m> as “the output variable revenue (R) in terms of the input variable price (p)”.  This often gets shortened to “R of p”, but notice it is a statement of assignment and not of multiplication.  
    </p>
    <example workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
            Consider the function <m>f(x)=x^2-x</m>, evaluate:

        </p>
        <sidebyside>
            <p>
                <m>f(3)</m>
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>f(-2)</m>
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>f\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)</m>
            </p>
            <p>
                <m>f(t)</m>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>
    </example>
